00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.990 align:start position:0%
 
two years ago today i released a video  that was ostensibly a letter to myself  10 years ago but was really an admission  that my mental health was not good and  in fact hadn't been good for quite some  time specifically i was suffering from  the effects of anxiety and depression  and if you'd like to learn more about my  specific case then i'll leave a link to  that video and all the videos i talk  about down in the description i followed  that up a year later with an update  talking about what i've been doing on my  mental health journey over the past year  and this video which i anticipate will  probably be the last in this mini series  seeks to continue that story what i've  been up to in terms of my mental health  over the past two years my reason for  making these videos is to try and expand  the conversation that we have about  mental health i think society's gotten a  lot better at openly discussing mental  health and people feel that they are  able to  say if they are suffering if they think  that they may be in trouble but what i  think is still lacking is the discussion  after that initial conversation  what happens next therapy medication  talking to medical professionals what  these videos this one and the previous  one are attempting to be is simply a  data point trying to show you what one  person's experience has been like  addressing their mental health over the  previous two years and perhaps you're  watching this as somebody who identifies  with some of the issues that i've  mentioned and you're curious as to what  might happen to you perhaps you're  watching this as somebody aiming to  support a friend or a family member i  hope that these videos give you some  kind of idea on both counts and spoiler  alerts they do feature something of a  happy outcome in this video i basically  want to talk about three things  medication therapy two things that kind  of defined the past year for me but also  some thoughts on trajectory and what  this all kind of means i've talked at  some length in the previous video about  medication but to very briefly summarize  after my initial video two years ago i  went to a gp and was prescribed  sertraline which is a drug that counters  both anxiety and depression and i took  sertraline for about 18 months i'm happy  to say that i am no longer taking  medication i'm happy to no longer be on  medication because whilst it absolutely  worked and it definitely made me less  anxious and it brought up my average  mood incidentally great timing to be  taking that during a global pandemic it  did have some side effects notably it  basically mellowed everything out  something that would have made me very  happy previously just registered  somewhere in the middle of my emotions  and equally something that was meant to  make me very sad would just register in  the middle of my emotions everything was  just sort of reduced to  shades of grey i absolutely think it was  the right decision to go on medication  and it definitely helped me through the  most difficult part of the process of  starting to address my mental health  those initial few months when i had to  answer myself some really difficult  questions i'm very glad that i was on  medication for that but at the same time  i know that it was the right decision to  come off of it because i felt like i  wanted to be myself again and after  stopping taking the medication i had  this wonderful feeling of  i could feel that in kind of intense joy  by watching your favorite movie or  listening to your favorite music and i  remember there was one day when i nearly  cried like three or four times because  stuff was sad and i remember being  really happy that i felt i was capable  of feeling that sad again ultimately  medications function in the arsenal of  dealing with mental health is to deal  with your symptoms it doesn't do  anything more than that it just  mitigates your symptoms and the idea was  always i would take medication until i  didn't need to anymore because the  underlying issues will have been if not  resolved addressed and hopefully  improved which has been happening over  the past year or so with  therapy the term therapy encompasses a  huge number of different practices and  they are all ultimately quite personal  so i am not going to go into huge detail  in terms of what i was dealing with  personally apologies if you came into  this video expecting tea uh i'm not  going to spill it but at any rate in my  case i went through two different kinds  of therapy i went through cbt or  cognitive behavioral therapy and i also  went through psychodynamic therapy cbt  was the therapy that was made available  to me on the nhs after an initial  consultation and personally i didn't  find it particularly useful it's  fantastic that it's made available for  free on the nhs but unfortunately for me  it didn't make much of a blasting impact  cbt is  kind of trying to rewire your brain it's  trying to address how you react to  certain stimuli in certain scenarios and  saying right okay well if this is making  you feel a particular way here's a tool  that will make you feel a different way  and i think for certain people that  would be very useful  again for me personally it didn't really  affect the way that i thought it didn't  really get to the heart of why i felt  the way i felt because of this i took  the decision to find and regularly meet  with a private therapist and i will say  up front that this was not cheap it was  50 pounds a session in my case and i  went to one session a week for four or  five months so you can do the maths on  how much money that cost me but  do i think it was one of the best  investments i've ever made  absolutely  yes i ended up working with a phenomenal  therapist called chris hi chris if  you're watching this he refused to watch  any of my videos whilst we were having  sessions together but he said that as  soon as i ended up moving to bath he was  going to find me on youtube and chris  was trained as a psychodynamicist which  is basically a way of looking at mental  health as a kind of chain of events  rather than another approach like  something like transactional analysis  it's quite similar actually to how i was  trained in atmospheric physics i was  trained as a dynamicist which means  looking at how the earth gets heated by  the sun and warms up and then that heats  the air above it and that air then moves  there's a kind of logical flow  it's a similar kind of thing with  psychodynamics as far as i can  understand the way that our sessions  worked was basically an hour-long  conversation with no particular goal in  mind it's not like we were working to a  syllabus or a timetable or anything and  what that practically meant was for the  first week or two just getting to know  me and what my head is doing at the  moment what the big events in my life  have been but after that inviting me to  talk about something initially and that  could be something that happened to me  that day or it might be something that  i'd mentioned in a previous session and  chris would effectively kind of guide  the conversation but let me speak for  myself and kind of draw my own  conclusions and add his  wisdom you know the information that  he's learned from his training to  my experiences my insights so he would  say for example that oh that sounds like  this person might be an example of a  transitional object and then talking  about what that means and what it's  meant for other people but at no point  was i told this is what this means this  is what your life has been doing it was  very much based on  letting me draw my own conclusions and  kind of letting me come to realizations  myself and this was a very private  process it's something that i didn't  even share with my fiance quite  frequently there were lots of things  that i wanted to keep for myself partly  for privacy but also because they were  so freshly formed in my head that i felt  like i couldn't articulate them yet and  it would take time to just sit in my  brain and feel like i knew  where i'd kind of gotten to what i'd  actually realized in my particular case  the ultimate realization i guess was  that  my anxiety which i think is the root  cause of an awful lot of my issues is  ultimately caused by a fear of  disappointing others  and  that's just something that's been  seared into me for half many years quite  possibly since childhood but recognizing  that process that idea makes a  tremendous difference in modifying my  behavior because ultimately the point of  therapy is to take subconscious  processes and just elevate them to the  conscious it's not actually to change  you it's not to rewire you it's just to  help you recognize  what's going on inside your head and  with that knowledge on with knowing that  you know this is the way my brain works  it modifies how i approach situations  and it has changed my behavior when i'm  in a situation where i'm feeling really  anxious about doing something for  somebody there's a little voice now in  my head that goes wait hang on no no  you're getting anxious because you're  afraid of disappointing them and they  they're not going to be disappointed  like because of these reasons and then  suddenly you know your shoulders untense  and you can you can breathe a little  easier and you just  generally calm down and that for me has  been the impact of therapy as i've  already said this process was expensive  and i am privileged to have been able to  afford it but if you are considering  therapy if you think it might benefit  you if you're on the fence i would  implore you to give it a go and i'd  implore you to find the right therapist  for you so mind which is a great charity  has a fantastic resource online for  finding a therapist near you  i was very lucky in that i actually  found chris on my first try and we just  kind of clicked it was the right person  for me but therapists recognize that you  have to click like that and it might not  be your first or second or third or  tenth therapist so a lot of people will  do a trial appointment and if it doesn't  work it doesn't work they won't be  offended and you can try with somebody  else the important thing is to feel  comfortable with whoever you are seeing  and that may be that you want to see  somebody in person at the moment it may  be that you want to only see somebody  via zoom over the internet whatever it  is that works for you but again if you  can afford it i would highly recommend  therapy it's  changed the trajectory of the rest of my  life which leads me on very neatly to  the last point i wanted to talk about  trajectory something that i think it's  important to understand is that mental  health doesn't have an end point there  is no point at which you are  fixed every single person on earth has  some form of internal struggle with  their emotions but that struggle can be  a minor skirmish from time to time or it  could be all-out war and in my case i  found that over the past two years the  scale of conflict has basically just  decreased it's not completely gone away  there are still days when i feel anxious  there are days when i feel depressed but  comparing the state now to how it was  two years ago there's just no comparison  i feel that i'm more in control of the  way that i feel i feel like i understand  more the way that i feel and i've  changed some of my habits to  force the conflict to de-escalate my  mental health journey isn't over the  past couple of years have basically been  just a chapter in that journey perhaps a  chapter at the end of a section of the  book actually we're now going into the  second part of that story whereas in  that first part i learned some skills  and i got some insight into the way that  my  emotions work the way that my brain  behaves and those insights and those  skills will be incredibly useful to me  and necessary to use for  the rest of my life through therapy i've  learned to elevate some of my  subconscious processes to conscious ones  i've also identified that i stress eat  and try to eat my way out of sadness you  may notice in this triptych videos that  i gained and then lost about 15 kilos i  learned that i previously undervalued  spending time away from my desk with  friends i undervalued time spent just  working on hobbies and reading and doing  exercise but these don't mean that i'm  somehow fixed they just mean that i'm  better equipped to deal with whatever  challenges come at me in the next  section the next part of my mental  health story and that just about wraps  up what i wanted to say in this video i  hope that this video and the previous  videos have been useful to you whether  you are perhaps undergoing a similar  kind of difficulties or if you are  caring for friends or family if you  found this video series and you're going  through a dark time in your life i want  you to know and i do mean you  specifically that  it's going to be all right  that  things pass  and eventually  they will get better  what you can do is to accelerate that  process by taking your mental health  seriously the first step is to  admit to yourself and perhaps to your  friends and to your family that things  aren't so great  the next step  is very individual but these videos have  shown you one possible route talking to  a medical professional taking medication  seeking therapy  and in this particular case  it worked and i went from an incredibly  dark place in my life to  feeling like a new person and feeling  much more content with  who i am  you can do it too  thank you for watching i'll leave links  to resources that you may find useful in  the description  and no matter where you are in your  mental health journey i hope that your  trajectory is curving upwards  that's the only important thing really  thank you for watching i'll see you in  the next one

